      "id": 1,
      "text": "Postmaster Charles Sauter named Sauk Village Strassburg after Strasbourg, France, where many early settlers came from.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sauk_Village%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before becoming a suburb, Sauk Village was a rural area of Bloom Township, with farms dating back to the 1838 New Strassburg settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Sauk_Village,_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A volunteer library started in Sauk Village, housed in the basement of Katz Corner School, before moving to a storefront in 1984.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sauk_Village%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Sauk Village sits atop the Tinley Moraine, a ridge formed by glacial activity during the last Ice Age.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sauk_Village%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area that is now Sauk Village was once home to several Native American tribes and lies on the high ground of the Valparaiso Moraine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sauk_Village%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 2012, Sauk Village voters chose to replace their local well water with Lake Michigan Water, ending a long‑standing water system.",
